(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_physics_waveoptics=[{id:"neet-2026-q013",pn:13,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Wave Optics",st:"Intensity in Young's double slit",q:"In Young's double slit experiment, using monochromatic light of wavelength \u03BB , the intensity of light at a point on the screen where the path difference is \u03BB , is K units. The intensity of light at a point where the path difference is \u03BB/3 will be",o:["(K)/(4)","K","(K)/(2)","2K"],c:0,s:`I = I\u2080cos\xB2(\u0394\u03C6/2), where I\u2080 is the maximum intensity.
I = I\u2080cos\xB2(K\u0394x/2), with wave number k = 2\u03C0/\u03BB.
For path difference \u03BB: K = I\u2080cos\xB2((2\u03C0/\u03BB) \xD7 (\u03BB/2)) = I\u2080cos\xB2\u03C0 = I\u2080.
For path difference \u03BB/3: K\u2081 = I\u2080cos\xB2((2\u03C0/\u03BB) \xD7 (\u03BB/(3\xD72))) = I\u2080cos\xB2(\u03C0/3).
K\u2081 = I\u2080/4.
So K/K\u2081 = 4, which gives K\u2081 = K/4.`},{id:"neet-2026-q034",pn:34,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Wave Optics",st:"Interference and diffraction",q:`In interference and diffraction, the light energy is redistributed. If it reduces in one region, producing a dark fringe, it increases in another region, producing a bright fringe.
A. As there is no gain or loss of energy, these phenomena are consistent with the principle of conservation of energy.
B. Diffraction and interference are characteristics exhibited only by light waves.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["A is true, but B is false","A is true and B is also true","A is false, but B is true","Both A and B are false"],c:0,s:`In interference and diffraction there is no loss of energy; the energy only gets redistributed, so A is true.
Interference and diffraction are shown by light waves as well as sound waves, so B is false.`},{id:"neet-2025-q027",pn:27,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Wave Optics",st:"Polarisation, Brewster's law",q:"An unpolarized light beam travelling in air is incident on a medium of refractive index 1.73 at Brewster\u2019s angle. Then",o:["Reflected light is completely polarized and the angle of reflection is close to 60\xB0","Reflected light is partially polarized and the angle of reflection is close to 30\xB0","Both reflected and transmitted light are perfectly polarized with angles of reflection and refraction close to 60\xB0 and 30\xB0, respectively","Transmitted light is completely polarized with angle of refraction close to 30\xB0"],c:0,s:`Use Brewster\u2019s law: \u03BC = tan\u03B8_P.
1.73 = tan\u03B8_P.
\u221A3 = tan\u03B8_P.
\u03B8_P = 60\xB0.
At this polarising angle, the reflected light is perfectly polarized and the transmitted light is partially polarised.`,sfig:{w:330,h:310,alt:"Light incident on a medium of index 1.73 at 60 degrees; reflected ray completely polarised, 90 degrees between reflected and transmitted rays, transmitted ray at 30 degrees partially polarised",d:[["l",32,157,290,157],["l",136,15,136,285],["t",142,24,"N"],["l",65,48,136,157,"a"],["t",5,36,"Incident"],["t",14,52,"beam"],["l",136,157,230,82,"a"],["t",176,69,"(completely polarised)","s"],["t",176,56,"Reflected light","s"],["l",136,157,182,270,"a"],["t",105,108,"60\xB0","s"],["t",146,108,"60\xB0","s"],["t",176,187,"90\xB0","s"],["t",184,167,"30\xB0","s"],["t",242,203,"\u03BC = 1.73"],["t",190,284,"Transmitted light","s"],["t",180,298,"(partially polarised)","s"],["p","M112 125 A 34 34 0 0 1 165 134"],["p","M170 154 A 34 34 0 0 1 150 188"],["l",34,172,288,172,"d"],["l",34,190,288,190,"d"],["l",34,208,288,208,"d"],["l",34,226,288,226,"d"],["l",34,244,288,244,"d"],["l",34,262,288,262,"d"]]}},{id:"neet-2025-q043",pn:43,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Wave Optics",st:"Polarisation, Malus law",q:"The intensity of transmitted light when a polaroid sheet, placed between two crossed polaroids at 22.5\xB0 from the polarization axis of one of the polaroids, is (I\u2080 is the intensity of polarised light after passing through the first polaroid):",o:["I\u2080/2","I\u2080/4","I\u2080/8","I\u2080/16"],c:2,s:`I\u2081 = I\u2080cos\xB2(45/2).
I\u2082 = I\u2081cos\xB2(90 \u2212 45/2).
I\u2082 = I\u2080cos\xB2(45/2) sin\xB2(45/2).
I\u2082 = (I\u2080/4)(4cos\xB2(45/2) sin\xB2(45/2)).
I\u2082 = (I\u2080/4) sin\xB245\xB0 = I\u2080/8.`,sfig:{w:450,h:190,alt:"Light along a dotted axis through three polaroids: first axis vertical, second tilted 22.5 degrees from vertical, third at 90 minus 22.5 degrees; intensities I0, I1, I2",d:[["l",4,107,448,107,"d"],["l",45,36,45,180,"b"],["l",164,42,164,182,"d"],["l",130,175,201,36,"b"],["p","M164 63 A24 24 0 0 1 187 70"],["t",175,28,"22.5\xB0"],["l",308,34,308,166,"d"],["l",279,166,323,74],["l",323,74,343,36,"d"],["l",256,82,386,140,"b"],["p","M323 76 A34 34 0 0 1 332 107"],["t",336,98,"90\xB0 \u2212 22.5\xB0"],["t",80,132,"I\u2080","s"],["t",226,132,"I\u2081","s"],["t",436,128,"I\u2082","s"]]}},{id:"neet-2024-q004",pn:4,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Wave Optics",st:"Polarisation by reflection, Brewster's angle",q:"An unpolarised light beam strikes a glass surface at Brewster's angle. Then",o:["both the reflected and refracted light will be completely polarised","the reflected light will be completely polarised but the refracted light will be partially polarised","the reflected light will be partially polarised","the refracted light will be completely polarised"],c:1,s:`At Brewster's angle the reflected light is completely polarised.
The refracted light is only partially polarised.`,sfig:{w:340,h:225,alt:"Unpolarised light incident on a glass surface at angle i: reflected ray at angle i is plane polarised (dots only); refracted ray at angle r is partially polarised (arrows and dots).",d:[["l",40,115,300,115],["l",170,15,170,225,"d"],[],["l",90,20,170,115],["l",170,115,250,20,"a"],["l",170,115,215,205,"a"],["l",92.7,37.2,106.5,25.6,"b"],["c",114,48.5,1.6,"F"],["l",121.5,71.4,135.3,59.8,"b"],["c",142.8,82.7,1.6,"F"],["l",147.1,101.8,160.9,90.2,"b"],["c",106,39,1.6,"F"],["c",122,58,1.6,"F"],["c",136.4,75.1,1.6,"F"],["c",149.2,90.3,1.6,"F"],["c",162,105.5,1.6,"F"],["c",187.9,93.4,1.8,"F"],["c",208.4,68.8,1.8,"F"],["c",228.9,44.2,1.8,"F"],["c",249.4,19.5,1.8,"F"],["l",173.2,141.5,189.3,133.5,"b"],["c",190.3,155.5,1.8,"F"],["l",191.2,177.5,207.3,169.5,"b"],["c",208.3,191.5,1.8,"F"],["p","M170 92 A23 23 0 0 0 154 98.5",""],["p","M170 92 A23 23 0 0 1 186 98.5",""],["p","M170 140 A25 25 0 0 0 180 138",""],["t",146,86,"i","e"],["t",190,86,"i"],["t",176,155,"r"],["t",95,18,"Incident rays"],["t",250,48,"Reflected rays"],["t",222,208,"Refracted rays"]]}},{id:"neet-2024-q010",pn:10,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Wave Optics",st:"Young's double slit with white light",q:"If the monochromatic source in Young's double slit experiment is replaced by white light, then-",o:["there will be a central bright white fringe surrounded by few coloured fringes","all bright fringes will be of equal width","interference pattern will disappear","there will be a central dark fringe surrounded by a few coloured fringes"],c:0,s:`At the central point the path difference is zero for every wavelength.
So the central fringe is bright and white.
Other fringes have width \u03B2 = \u03BBD/d, which depends on wavelength, so they are coloured.`},{id:"neet-2023-q022",pn:22,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Wave Optics",st:"Young's double slit experiment",q:`For Young's double slit experiment, two statements are given below:
Statement I: If screen is moved away from the plane of slits, angular separation of the fringes remains constant.
Statement II: If the monochromatic source is replaced by another monochromatic source of larger wavelength, the angular separation of fringes decreases.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["Statement I is false but Statement II is true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are false.","Statement I is true but Statement II is false."],c:3,s:`Angular fringe width \u03B1 = \u03BB/d.
It does not depend on the screen distance, so Statement I is true.
\u03B1 is proportional to \u03BB, so a larger \u03BB increases the angular separation.
Statement II says it decreases, so it is false.`},{id:"neet-2022-q024",pn:24,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Wave Optics",st:"Fringe width",q:"In a Young\u2019s double slit experiment, a student observes 8 fringes in a certain segment of screen when a monochromatic light of 600 nm wavelength is used. If the wavelength of light is changed to 400 nm, then the number of fringes he would observe in the same region of the screen is",o:["8","9","12","6"],c:2,s:`Fringe width \u03B2 = \u03BBD/d.
Let the segment length be \u2113.
\u2113 = 8\u03B2\u2081 = 8\u03BB\u2081D/d and \u2113 = n\u03B2\u2082 = n\u03BB\u2082D/d.
So 8\u03BB\u2081 = n\u03BB\u2082, i.e. 8 \xD7 600 = n \xD7 400.
n = 12.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q025",pn:25,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Wave Optics",st:"Young's double slit fringe width",q:"If the screen is moved away from the plane of the slits in a Young's double slit experiment, then the:",o:["linear separation of the fringes decreases","angular separation of the fringes increases","angular separation of the fringes decreases","linear separation of the fringes increases"],c:3,s:`Fringe width \u03B2 = (\u03BBD)/(d).
When D increases, \u03B2 increases.
So the linear separation of the fringes increases.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q030",pn:30,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Wave Optics",st:"Malus law",q:"After passing through a polariser a linearly polarised light of intensity I is incident on an analyser making an angle of 30\xB0 with that of the polariser. The intensity of light emitted from the analyser will be:",o:["2I/3","I/2","I/3","3I/4"],c:3,s:`Use Malus' law: I' = I cos\xB2\u03B8.
I' = I cos\xB230\xB0 = I \xD7 3/4 = 3I/4.`},{id:"neet-2020-q110",pn:110,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Wave Optics",st:"Fringe width",q:"In Young's double slit experiment, if the separation between coherent sources is halved and the distance of the screen from the coherent sources is doubled, then the fringe width becomes :",o:["half","four times","one-fourth","double"],c:1,s:`Fringe width \u03B2 = \u03BBD/d.
New D = 2D and new d = d/2.
\u03B2' = \u03BB(2D)/(d/2) = 4\u03BBD/d = 4\u03B2.
The fringe width becomes four times.`},{id:"neet-2020-q125",pn:125,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Wave Optics",st:"Brewster angle",q:"The Brewster's angle i_b for an interface should be :",o:["30\xB0 < i_b < 45\xB0","45\xB0 < i_b < 90\xB0","i_b = 90\xB0","0\xB0 < i_b < 30\xB0"],c:1,s:`tan i_b = \u03BC\u2082/\u03BC\u2081 (relative refractive index).
For an interface with \u03BC\u2082 > \u03BC\u2081 the ratio is greater than 1.
So tan i_b > 1, which gives 45\xB0 < i_b < 90\xB0.`},{id:"neet-2019-q126",pn:126,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Wave Optics",st:"Young's double slit",q:"In a double slit experiment, when light of wavelength 400 nm was used, the angular width of the first minima formed on a screen placed 1 m away, was found to be 0.2\xB0. What will be the angular width of the first minima. If the entire experimental apparatus is immersed in water ? (\u03BCwater = 4/3)",o:["0.05\xB0","0.1\xB0","0.266\xB0","0.15\xB0"],c:3,s:`Angular width \u03B8 = \u03BB/d, so \u03B8 \u221D \u03BB.
In water \u03BB\u2032 = \u03BB/\u03BC = \u03BB/(4/3) = 3\u03BB/4.
\u03B8\u2032 = (3/4) \xD7 0.2\xB0 = 0.15\xB0.`},{id:"neet-2018-q031",pn:31,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Wave Optics",st:"Polarisation by reflection, Brewster's law",q:"Unpolarised light is incident from air on a plane surface of a material of refractive index '\u03BC'. At a particular angle of incidence 'i', it is found that the reflected and refracted rays are perpendicular to each other. Which of the following options is correct for this situation ?",o:["Reflected light is polarised with its electric vector parallel to the plane of incidence","Reflected light is polarised with its electric vector perpendicular to the plane of incidence","i = sin\u207B\xB9((1)/(\u03BC))","i = tan\u207B\xB9((1)/(\u03BC))"],c:1,s:`Reflected and refracted rays at 90\xB0 is the condition of Brewster's angle.
At this angle the reflected light is completely plane polarised.
Its electric vector is perpendicular to the plane of incidence.
Also tan i = \u03BC, so i = tan\u207B\xB9(\u03BC), which rules out options (3) and (4).`,sfig:{w:260,h:195,alt:"Unpolarised light incident on a surface between air and a medium; reflected and refracted rays are perpendicular, reflected ray polarised.",d:[["l",10,100,240,100,""],["t",235,86,"Air","e"],["t",235,120,"Medium (\u03BC)","e"],["l",23,41.3,110,100,""],["l",82.3,81.3,88.9,85.8,"a"],["l",110,100,192.9,44.1,""],["l",177.2,54.7,183.8,50.2,"a"],["l",110,100,163.1,178.8,""],["l",152.9,163.6,157.4,170.3,"a"],["c",94.3,89.4,2.2,"F"],["l",89.3,96.9,99.4,82,""],["p","M89.3 96.9L93.2 94.7L89.9 92.5Z","F"],["p","M99.4 82L95.5 84.2L98.8 86.4Z","F"],["c",75.2,76.5,2.2,"F"],["l",70.1,84,80.2,69.1,""],["p","M70.1 84L74 81.8L70.7 79.5Z","F"],["p","M80.2 69.1L76.3 71.3L79.6 73.5Z","F"],["c",134.9,83.2,2.2,"F"],["c",155.6,69.2,2.2,"F"],["c",121.7,117.3,2.2,"F"],["l",114.2,122.4,129.1,112.3,""],["p","M114.2 122.4L118.7 121.8L116.4 118.5Z","F"],["p","M129.1 112.3L124.7 112.9L127 116.2Z","F"],["c",136.6,139.4,2.2,"F"],["l",129.1,144.4,144,134.3,""],["p","M129.1 144.4L133.5 143.8L131.3 140.5Z","F"],["p","M144 134.3L139.6 134.9L141.8 138.2Z","F"]]}},{id:"neet-2018-q032",pn:32,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Wave Optics",st:"Fringe angular width",q:"In Young's double slit experiment the separation d between the slits is 2 mm, the wavelength \u03BB of the light used is 5896 \xC5 and distance D between the screen and slits is 100 cm. It is found that the angular width of the fringes is 0.20\xB0. To increase the fringe angular width to 0.21\xB0 (with same \u03BB and D) the separation between the slits needs to be changed to",o:["1.8 mm","1.9 mm","2.1 mm","1.7 mm"],c:1,s:`Angular fringe width \u03B8 = (\u03BB)/(d), so \u03B8 \u221D (1)/(d) for fixed \u03BB.
(0.20\xB0)/(0.21\xB0) = (d\u2081)/(d), where d = 2 mm.
d\u2081 = (0.20 \xD7 2)/(0.21) = (0.40)/(0.21) = 1.9 mm.`},{id:"neet-2017-q139",pn:139,y:"NEET 2017 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Wave Optics",st:"Polarisation and Malus' law",q:"Two Polaroids P1 and P2 are placed with their axis perpendicular to each other. Unpolarised light I0 is incident on P1. A third polaroid P3 is kept in between P1 and P2 such that its axis makes an angle 45\xBA with that of P1. The intensity of transmitted light through P2 is",o:["I0/16","I0/2","I0/4","I0/8"],c:3,s:`Unpolarised light through P1 gives I0/2.
P3 is at 45\xB0 to P1: by Malus' law I = (I0/2) cos\xB245\xB0 = I0/4.
P2 is at 90\xB0 to P1, so it is at 45\xB0 to P3: I = (I0/4) cos\xB245\xB0 = I0/8.`},{id:"neet-2017-q176",pn:176,y:"NEET 2017 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Wave Optics",st:"Young's double slit",q:"Young's double slit experiment is first performed in air and then in a medium other than air. It is found that 8th bright fringe in the medium lies where 5th dark fringe lies in air. The refractive index of the medium is nearly :",o:["1.78","1.25","1.59","1.69"],c:0,s:`In air, the 5th dark fringe is at y = (2\xD75 \u2212 1)\u03BBD/(2d) = 9\u03BBD/(2d).
In the medium, wavelength is \u03BB/\u03BC, so the 8th bright fringe is at y = 8\u03BBD/(\u03BCd).
Equating: 8/\u03BC = 9/2, so \u03BC = 16/9 \u2248 1.78.`}]})();
